WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:04.180
Element 85 is astatine.

00:00:04.880 --> 00:00:08.220
Astatine filled in the space at the bottom of the halogens.

00:00:08.440 --> 00:00:12.520
Now, of course, it's not at the bottom of the halogens now

00:00:12.620 --> 00:00:16.180
because very recently Tennessine
went below it.

00:00:21.660 --> 00:00:24.220
I'm not sure that I would drink it all the time.

00:00:24.460 --> 00:00:29.980
But at the time it was discovered it was the heaviest halogen.

00:00:29.980 --> 00:00:34.320
That is in the group that begins with fluorine, and goes down

00:00:34.320 --> 00:00:37.700
chlorine, bromine, iodine, astatine.

00:00:37.860 --> 00:00:44.800
Astatine was known to be, or expected to be really radioactive.

00:00:44.960 --> 00:00:49.180
Now they knew there was an element (that) should be there for two reasons:

00:00:49.180 --> 00:00:56.280
first of all because if you looked at the periodic table there appeared to be a hole, but that's not terribly scientific.

00:00:56.280 --> 00:01:02.360
But using the experiments that had been done in the early 20th century

00:01:02.360 --> 00:01:09.780
by a physicist called Henry Moseley, they knew that by the x-ray emissions of the different elements

00:01:09.780 --> 00:01:15.160
there ought to be an element between polonium and radon.

00:01:15.160 --> 00:01:21.979
Searches for astatine in nature failed. There was no obvious traces of this

00:01:21.979 --> 00:01:27.800
missing radioactive element, so in the end it was decided that it should be

00:01:27.800 --> 00:01:34.900
synthesized by taking bismuth, which is element 83,

00:01:34.900 --> 00:01:44.480
and bombarding it with alpha particles. Alpha particles are nuclei of helium with element number 2.

00:01:44.480 --> 00:01:55.399
83 plus 2 makes 85, so you imagine the accelerated alpha particles hitting the nuclei of

00:01:55.400 --> 00:02:03.300
the bismuth and eventually one of them will fuse and you will get an atom of astatine.

00:02:03.300 --> 00:02:10.260
Now this is similar to the more modern approach which has been used for the super-heavy elements,

00:02:10.260 --> 00:02:13.660
but astatine is much more stable than those,

00:02:13.660 --> 00:02:17.760
so once you made an atom it didn't decay immediately.

00:02:17.760 --> 00:02:21.480
So you could build up modest quantities of astatine,

00:02:21.480 --> 00:02:27.480
but not nearly enough to actually see any of the solid material.

00:02:27.620 --> 00:02:36.760
After astatine had been discovered, or synthesized, it was then discovered in traces amounts in minerals.

00:02:36.760 --> 00:02:42.520
Once you know the emission signature of the radioactive particles coming out from its decay,

00:02:42.520 --> 00:02:46.160
you can detect those emissions elsewhere.

00:02:46.160 --> 00:02:52.460
And it's been calculated that if you took all the astatine that exists

00:02:52.460 --> 00:02:59.960
in the whole world at any one time it would weigh less than these three coins.

00:02:59.960 --> 00:03:09.500
So that's less than 30 grams -- less than an ounce -- in the whole world, and none of it is in lumps.

00:03:09.500 --> 00:03:13.860
There's the odd atom here or there which decays quite rapidly.

00:03:13.860 --> 00:03:23.680
For me, one of the most interesting questions is whether astatine behaves like other halogens.

00:03:23.680 --> 00:03:28.159
If you go down the group you would expect because fluorine and

00:03:28.159 --> 00:03:33.170
chlorine are gasses, bromine is a liquid, iodine is a solid, you would expect that

00:03:33.170 --> 00:03:40.020
astatine would be a solid, though like francium, its radioactivity might blow the solid apart,

00:03:40.020 --> 00:03:46.699
but the other point about astatine is that as you go down

00:03:46.699 --> 00:03:53.180
these elements, they gradually become more and more like a metal.

00:03:53.180 --> 00:04:01.540
So iodine is strongly colored and sometimes forms positive ions as well as negative ions.

00:04:01.540 --> 00:04:08.090
Astatine could have some metallic characters and in chemistry it sometimes

00:04:08.090 --> 00:04:14.959
seems to resemble polonium, the element next to it (element number 84) more than

00:04:14.959 --> 00:04:18.240
it does resemble iodine, the element above.

00:04:18.240 --> 00:04:26.400
Perhaps the most interesting fact, which i think is quite fun, is that for all the other halogens

00:04:26.400 --> 00:04:35.690
if you form the compound with hydrogen, hydrogen chloride, hydrogen iodide, the halogen is negative

00:04:35.690 --> 00:04:38.820
with respect to the hydrogen, and you get an acid.

00:04:38.820 --> 00:04:44.240
And it has been argued that with astatine it may be the other way round.

00:04:44.240 --> 00:04:51.240
It may be "astatine hydride," rather than "hydrogen astatide."

00:04:51.240 --> 00:04:58.660
The reason that it was called astatine is because it comes from the Greek word astatos (αστατος),

00:04:58.660 --> 00:05:03.500
which means "unstable." I think the pronunciation is right,

00:05:03.500 --> 00:05:11.060
I asked one of my Greek colleagues at coffee today, and in fact in Greek it is still called astatos.

00:05:11.060 --> 00:05:17.660
It means unstable in the sense that the weather is changeable or something like that,

00:05:17.660 --> 00:05:22.260
so it's quite good, because I think if you think about it chemically,

00:05:22.260 --> 00:05:28.800
first of all it means it's unstable, it decays, but it also means perhaps it's unstable,

00:05:28.800 --> 00:05:31.260
it's not quite sure whether it's a metal or a halogen.

00:05:31.260 --> 00:05:33.960
Well, before I started making these videos,

00:05:33.960 --> 00:05:38.340
I thought all these elements were very boring and never really thought about them.

00:05:38.340 --> 00:05:43.580
What I now discover is that each of these elements when you start delving into it

00:05:43.580 --> 00:05:50.180
is actually quite an interesting story, and particularly they're interesting stories

00:05:50.180 --> 00:05:54.560
of human ambition and greed among the people

00:05:54.560 --> 00:05:59.180
who want to claim to have discovered it, and there are whole series of people.

00:05:59.180 --> 00:06:05.280
One unsuccessful claimant wanted to call it "alabamine," after Alabama,

00:06:05.280 --> 00:06:12.820
presumably his home state, so it's quite fun that the element that is now underneath it,

00:06:12.820 --> 00:06:16.480
element 117, is called Tennessine.

00:06:16.480 --> 00:06:26.640
So in the end, an American state got recognition. Though, sadly for that chemist, it wasn't Alabama.

00:06:29.780 --> 00:06:33.560
...and not only Destin, who'd be quite exciting in his own right.

00:06:33.560 --> 00:06:39.060
And so we're really interested. He's not a very good actor, is he?

00:06:39.060 --> 00:06:45.100
I'm so happy! (laughs) I can't believe... I'm sorry, go ahead.

00:06:45.100 --> 00:06:48.340
(Explosion)(Laughter)

